2026-07-27T03:01:09.2047079Z             "body": "## Summary\nFixes a security issue where the `ZoneTransferCheck` was not respecting the `--no-direct-dns` flag, causing raw TCP/53 zone transfer attempts to be sent to authoritative nameservers even when direct DNS was disabled. This check now properly gates itself like all other direct-DNS checks.\n\n## Changes\n- **ZoneTransferCheck**: Added a gate that skips the check when `EnableDirectDns` is false, returning a skipped result before any network operations occur\n- **Web UI**: Updated the tooltip text for the \"Direct DNS / external resolvers\" option to clarify that AXFR checks are skipped when disabled\n- **Tests**: Added comprehensive `DirectDnsGatingTests` class that:\n  - Verifies zone transfer is skipped when `EnableDirectDns` is disabled\n  - Confirms zone transfer still runs when `EnableDirectDns` is enabled (control test)\n  - Tests all seven direct-DNS checks (propagation, lame delegation, SOA serial, glue records, parent delegation, open resolver, AXFR) to ensure they all properly skip when the flag is disabled\n\n## Implementation Details\nThe fix adds a simple guard clause at the beginning of `ZoneTransferCheck.RunAsync()` that checks `ctx.Options.EnableDirectDns` before any network operations. This matches the pattern already implemented in the other six direct-DNS checks. The test suite uses a cached AXFR verdict to verify the check can reach a definitive result offline, ensuring the skip behavior comes from the gate rather than missing data.\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CsVVeUTFGF1xXBej8jFcY9",
